**To verify a copy**, fetch `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RPi-Distro/firmware-nonfree/bookworm/<upstream path>`
and compare the SHA-256. Nothing here has to be trusted on this project's word.

**`-standard` rather than `-minimal`, and what the difference actually is.** Upstream ships both
(548,728 and 609,309 bytes) and its own `cypress/README.txt` says what the smaller one gives up. It is
not a compressed equivalent: `-minimal` REMOVES features to free memory so the chip can hold more client
state in ACCESS-POINT mode - about 19 clients - namely

  * 802.11k / 802.11v / 802.11r advanced roaming,
  * `dfsradar`, which lets an AP use channels shared with radar systems,
  * `obss-obssdump`, automatic channel selection,
  * `swdiv`, antenna diversity - and upstream notes this is irrelevant with a single antenna anyway.

Worth noticing: **none of those matter to GodspeedOS**, because `docs/wifi.md` section 9 rules out AP
mode and roaming as non-goals, and the Pi 4 has one antenna. So `-minimal` would very likely work here
too. `-standard` is taken because it is the full part and the default the Pi distribution installs, and
there is no reason to accept a cut-down build when the thing it buys - AP client capacity - is something
this OS does not do. Chosen on what the variants DO, not on their size.
